        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A whole house humidifier is the right answer for a ducted house in a cold climate that reads dry on a hygrometer all winter. A bypass unit suits most gas-furnace homes at the lowest cost, a fan-powered unit fits where a bypass will not, and a steam unit is the choice for heat pumps and homes that need serious output.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever you choose, the setpoint matters more than the model. Lower it as the outdoor temperature drops, replace the water panel every fall, and treat fogged windows as a warning. Done that way, it is one of the most hands-off comfort upgrades a house can get.
          </p>
